package ferrum:plugin@1.0.0;

/// A plugin that can bring what it shows up to date.
///
/// Optional, and separate from `lifecycle` for the reason `worlds.wit` gives
/// about every provider export: a world that made this mandatory would stop a
/// component that does not implement it from instantiating at all. A plugin
/// whose reading never goes stale — a command handler, a linter — implements
/// nothing here and is unaffected by this file existing.
///
/// # Why the host needs to ask at all
///
/// `activate` used to be the only call into a guest anywhere in this world.
/// Everything a plugin displayed was therefore computed once, when the editor
/// started, and then never again. That is invisible while a reading is static
/// and wrong the moment it is not: a status item showing a figure from an hour
/// ago looks exactly like one showing a figure from now.
///
/// The host decides when. A guest cannot wake itself up — there is no timer in
/// this world and deliberately so, because a plugin that could schedule its own
/// work could spend the editor's frame budget without anybody granting it
/// anything.
interface refresher {
  /// Recompute whatever this plugin shows.
  ///
  /// Called on the host's own schedule and possibly never. It should be cheap
  /// and it should be safe to call when nothing has changed: the host does not
  /// know what "changed" means for any particular plugin, so it does not try to
  /// guess and instead asks.
  ///
  /// Returns nothing. A plugin that cannot recompute should keep showing what
  /// it has rather than clearing itself — a blank reading and a stale one are
  /// both wrong, and the stale one at least says when it was taken.
  refresh: async func();
}
